  "query_preview": "/Tuesday through Saturday/... \nConditions will remain favorable through the middle of the week \nfor additional monsoonal activity. Depending on how active Monday \nturns out, Tuesday could actually be more of a down day given a \nworked over atmosphere and thus even though there is still \npotential for storm activity, especially across the higher terrain\nareas, NBM PoPs for Tuesday are lower, mostly ranging between \n30-50%. Another more active period appears to be in the cards for \nlate Wednesday into the first half of Thursday. The flow by \nWednesday is forecast to shift out of the south as the subtropical\nhigh shifts into Texas and a upper-level low moves off the coast \nof California. This southerly flow is likely to bring additional \nmoisture northward from Mexico, with projected PWATs increasing to\n1.7-2.0\" and low-level mixing ratios increasing to 12-13 g/kg. In\naddition, there will likely more favorable upper-level forcing as\nboth the GFS and ECMWF do indicate an upper-level wave moving \ninto the region from Mexico, which will likely enhance the \nconvective activity with flash flooding becoming more of a threat \ngiven the deeper moisture profiles that will be in place. The \nlatest NBM PoPs for late Wednesday through the first part of \nThursday currently range between 50-60% across much of south- \ncentral and southeast AZ. \n\nHeading towards the end of the week and next weekend, the guidance \nis in good agreement in showing the subtropical high shifting \ntowards the Gulf Coast Region while the aforementioned upper- \nlevel low remains situated off the west coast. This pattern \nconfiguration will cause the flow to shift more out of the \nsouthwest, bringing in drier air as PWATs drop under 1.2-1.3\". As \na result, an overall downtrend in storm activity is likely. \n\nWith the lowering heights aloft and as well as an increase in \nconvective activity and cloud cover, temperatures throughout the \nupcoming work week are expected to gradually cool with afternoon \nhighs expected to fall to near seasonal levels by Tuesday and \npotentially a few degrees below normal by the latter half of the \nweek. Temperatures do eventually trend slightly back up again by\nnext weekend, but to near seasonal levels with no extreme heat in\nsight.",
